      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the property that if two triangles are similar, then their corresponding sides are proportional. Given that \\(\\triangle PAB \\sim \\triangle PCA\\), we can set up the following proportion:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PA}{PC} = \\frac{PB}{PA} = \\frac{AB}{CA}\n\\]\n\nGiven the side lengths of \\(\\triangle ABC\\):\n- \\(AB = 8\\)\n- \\(BC = 7\\)\n- \\(CA = 6\\)\n\nWe need to find the length of \\(PC\\). Let's denote \\(PC\\) as \\(x\\). Since \\(AB = 8\\) and \\(CA = 6\\), the proportion simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PA}{PC} = \\frac{PB}{PA} = \\frac{8}{6} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe know \\(PB = PB\\) and \\(PA = PA\\) are some fixed lengths, but for our proportion, we only need to use the ratio of the sides. From the proportion \\(\\frac{PA}{PC} = \\frac{4}{3}\\), we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PA}{x} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\n\\[\n3PA = 4x\n\\]\n\nWe also know from the similarity that \\(PB = PA\\), so:\n\n\\[\nPB = PA\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PB\\) is a part of \\(PC\\) and \\(PB + AC = PC\\), we have:\n\n\\[\nPB + 6 = x\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(PB = PA\\) into the proportion, we get:\n\n\\[\nPA = \\frac{4}{3}x\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA + PB = PC\\):\n\n\\[\nPA + PA = PC\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2PA = PC\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(PA = \\frac{4}{3}x\\) into the equation \\(2PA = PC\\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\left(\\frac{4}{3}x\\right) = x\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{8}{3}x = x\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we multiply both sides by 3:\n\n\\[\n8x = 3x\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(3x\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n5x = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx = 9\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\(PC\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we check the consistency with the given side lengths and the similarity condition. The correct answer is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
