      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to use the properties of similar triangles. We are given that \\(\\triangle PAB \\sim \\triangle PCA\\). This implies that the corresponding sides of these triangles are proportional. Let's denote the length of \\(PC\\) as \\(x\\). We need to find the value of \\(x\\).\n\nFirst, let's write down the ratios of the corresponding sides of the similar triangles \\(\\triangle PAB \\sim \\triangle PCA\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PA}{PC} = \\frac{PB}{PA} = \\frac{AB}{CA}\n\\]\n\nWe know the lengths of the sides \\(AB = 8\\), \\(BC = 7\\), and \\(CA = 6\\). Let \\(PB = y\\). Since \\(BC\\) is extended to point \\(P\\), we have \\(PB = PC + BC = x + 7\\). Therefore, \\(y = x + 7\\).\n\nNow, let's substitute the known values into the ratio of the sides:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{PA}{x} = \\frac{y}{PA} = \\frac{8}{6} = \\frac{4}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two equations:\n\n1. \\(\\frac{PA}{x} = \\frac{4}{3}\\)\n2. \\(\\frac{y}{PA} = \\frac{4}{3}\\)\n\nFrom the first equation, we can express \\(PA\\) in terms of \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nPA = \\frac{4}{3}x\n\\]\n\nFrom the second equation, we can express \\(y\\) in terms of \\(PA\\):\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{4}{3}PA\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(y = x + 7\\) into the second equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nx + 7 = \\frac{4}{3}(PA)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA = \\frac{4}{3}x\\), we substitute this into the equation:\n\n\\[\nx + 7 = \\frac{4}{3} \\left( \\frac{4}{3}x \\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\nx + 7 = \\frac{16}{9}x\n\\]\n\nTo clear the fraction, multiply every term by 9:\n\n\\[\n9x + 63 = 16x\n\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n63 = 16x - 9x\n\\]\n\n\\[\n63 = 7x\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{63}{7} = 9\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of \\(PC\\) is \\(9\\).\n\nThe correct option is \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
